+# Native gateway plugin controls
+
+An in-process plugin can register a control that remains available during an agent turn:
+
+```python
+def register(ctx):
+    def control(raw_args):
+        proof = ctx.get_command_context()
+        if proof is None:
+            return "Control unavailable."
+        # Compare proof.source and proof.admitted_ingress.message against the
+        # ORIGINAL task owner (bot/user/chat/topic/profile) before any action.
+        return controller.control(raw_args, proof)
+
+    ctx.register_command("task-control", control, gateway_control=True)
+```
+
+The owning runtime profile must explicitly grant
+`plugins.entries.<plugin-id>.allow_gateway_control: true` in its `config.yaml`.
+Registration alone grants no authority. Consent is reread on every dispatch.
+Existing commands without `gateway_control=True` retain their idle behavior.
+Built-in commands such as `/stop`, `/pause` and `/status` cannot be shadowed.
+Registered control names remain reserved for the lifetime of their profile's
+plugin manager. After disposal or unload, fresh commands are refused rather
+than becoming agent input. Re-registering a control restores its checked path;
+downgrading that name to an ordinary command does not bypass this reservation.
+
+`ctx.get_command_context()` returns a detached plain dictionary only inside this
+plugin's authenticated command invocation. Its `source` contains `platform`,
+`user_id`, `chat_id`, `chat_type`, `thread_id` and explicit runtime `profile`.
+`session_key` and `command` identify the native command lane.
+`admitted_ingress` contains the canonical transport/runtime profile and original
+native message proof (`bot_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `thread_id`, `message_id`,
+`platform_update_id`, reply and media provenance). No SDK objects, credentials,
+conversation text or arguments are copied into the context. `raw_args` remains
+an ordinary handler argument. The public `get_command_context()` accessor in
+`hermes_cli.plugin_command_context` exposes the same invocation-local receipt.
+
+The gateway supplies the receipt only after native user, bot and slash admission,
+and only with a non-inferred routing identity and proof from the actual receiving
+adapter. Missing provenance, a foreign profile, revoked consent, shutdown or an
+exception consumes the control without invoking the model. Native task ownership
+is separate: plugins must verify their own original task owner and budget. A
+receipt is not a capability to control another task or start another session.
+
+Controls bypass both native busy guards inline, after the existing
+emergency-pause policy; draining refuses dispatch
+and revokes an invocation's context. No new pause exception is introduced.
+Async handlers run on the gateway loop; sync handlers use the existing gateway
+executor with copied ContextVars.
+Blocking inference does not occupy the only control worker: that native executor
+is unbounded. Handlers must remain bounded and return promptly. The context is
+reset on success, cancellation and failure; inherited tasks lose access after the
+invocation ends. Saved receipt dictionaries are snapshots, not renewable authority.
+
+The isolated plugin host cannot register gateway controls or obtain native
+command context. The host audit reports these surfaces as in-process only.
